    "description": "Meridian's desktop client currently performs separate remote requests to inventory availability, pricing, credit validation, and order persistence. A single checkout action therefore produces dozens of network calls, repeated authentication checks, and inconsistent partial results when a later request fails. The Merchandise service owns rich Order, SKU, Customer, and Pricing aggregates whose small methods must remain usable only inside that service. We need to expose one coarse SubmitOrder operation that gathers all required input, performs the existing use case locally, and returns one durable order identifier plus any rejection details. The network adapter may coordinate calls to internal collaborators but must not contain pricing, credit, or inventory rules. Its request and response need stable serializable shapes that do not expose the internal object graph, and the desktop client must be able to complete checkout in one remote round trip.",
    "decision_statement": "Decide where to place a coarse, domain-free remote interface that lets one client action complete checkout in a single round trip while the service's fine-grained domain objects remain internal.",
    "notes": "remote-facade fits the coarse SubmitOrder operation collapsing dozens of calls into one round trip; data-transfer-object and mapper cover the payload-shape and translation readings."
